    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • Ford Rolls Out the Ranger MS-RT Champion Edition: You Can Choose Any Color As Long as It Is Black\nBugatti La Maison Pur Sang Redoes an Aluminum-Bodied Veyron Grand Sport Vitesse\n13 photos\nPhoto: Bugatti\nThere are only three main models presently offered by Bugatti to its customers: the Tourbillon, W16 Mistral, and Bolide, and for most people who can afford any of these, that’s more than enough. There are some, however, that look for older models, especially the ones that are unique in one form or another. And this is where La Maison Pur Sang comes in.\nThe name hides, quite skillfully, Bugatti's official in-house heritage, restoration, and certification program, meaning the people tasked with digging up past Bugattis and bringing them back into the spotlight. And their most recent stunt is worthy of its 15 minutes of fame, again.\nA little more than a decade ago, an unnamed customer with a passion for highly individualized cars asked Bugatti for a special version of the Veyron Grand Sport Vitesse, or something that’s nothing short of a work of art: an all-aluminum-bodied hypercar meant to shame other hypercars in all respects\nBugatti delivered, of course, and made the car’s almost entire bodywork from exposed polished aluminum. It needed quite a lot of metal of this kind: the carmaker says its people required massive blocks of aluminum that at times weighed up to 20 tons.\nLeft raw, the shiny stuff makes up the side panels in their entirety, with only small patches of exposed carbon fiber that can be seen here and there. At the same time, the interior leather has been specified in Carmine Red to deliver one of the most impactful contrasts known to man.\nThe roadster is known internally as chassis 8.100 and is animated by Bugatti’s 8.0-liter quad-turbocharged W16 engine that delivers 1,184 hp and 1,500 Nm of torque. What a sight this thing might be when going down the road...\nThe aluminum-bodied Veyron Grand Sport Vitesse is back in the spotlight after La Maison Pur Sang announced it has completed the refurbishment, reconfiguration, and certification process for the one-off, before sending it to its new owner on the French Riviera.\nWe’re told work on what is described as one of the most ambitious projects of the Veyron era was conducted using the documentation created back when the car was originally put together.\nThe focus of the team that did the work was to restore it to its original self, but Bugatti does not say what happened to it since it first rolled off the assembly lines to require such a thing. The only thing we’re informed on is that the process required the removal of later personalization elements, but no specifics are provided.\nWe also don’t know how much the thing cost to put back together, but word in the industry is that it originally cost more than double the price of a stock Veyron, which was around $2.5 million.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nBugatti Bugatti Veyron Grand Sport Vitesse La Maison Pur Sang\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Daniel Patrascu\nDaniel loves writing (or so he claims), and he uses this skill to offer readers a \"behind the scenes\" look at the automotive industry. He also enjoys talking about space exploration and robots, because in his view the only way forward for humanity is away from this planet, in metal bodies. Full profile\nUP NEXT First Sneak Peek at the Porsche Mission S Just Before You're Supposed To See It",
